lack bean. - Reiterate that this is why these products are white and cheap, unlike real vanilla bean products. End on a friendly, clarifying note.
00:04:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really logical assumption, and I can see why you'd make it—if vanillin is the flavor molecule from vanilla, it must come from the bean, just extracted or processed differently. It’s like saying sugar comes from sugar cane, even if it’s refined. But here’s the surprising bit: **the vast majority of vanillin used in "vanilla flavored" foods does *not* come from vanilla beans at all.** - **Synthetic vanillin** (the most common source) is made from petrochemicals or from lignin, a compound found in wood pulp. It’s chemically identical to the main flavor molecule in real vanilla, but never touches a vanilla orchid. Less than 1% of the world’s vanillin supply is actually extracted from vanilla beans. - **Natural vanillin** can also be produced by fermentation of ferulic acid (from rice bran, cloves, or other plants), and this is still *not* from vanilla beans, though it can be labeled "natural flavor" in some contexts. - Only **vanilla extract** or **vanilla bean preparations** actually come from vanilla beans, and they contain hundreds of other compounds alongside vanillin, which gives real vanilla its complex, rounded flavor. So when a plain white yogurt says "vanilla flavored" and has zero specks, it's almost certainly using synthetic or non-vanilla-derived vanillin—which is clear/white, cheap, and entirely bean-free. That’s why it doesn’t carry the color, the cost, or the specks of the real thing. Your logic holds perfectly for true **vanilla bean** products (they cost more, have specks, and the flavor is genuinely bean-derived). But the jump to "all vanilla flavor must be from beans" is where the food industry has pulled a massive switcheroo: they've made "vanilla" synonymous with the single molecule vanillin, regardless of its origin, so most of the time, there’s literally no bean involved at all. It's a sneaky little truth, and you're not wrong to question it—it should be more transparent.
